## Modeling Diverse Animals

Different animals need different data. An enum lets each variant carry its own unique set of fields.

### The Animal Enum

```xiom
enum Animal {
  Lion(age: Int),
  Elephant(age: Int, weight: Float64),
  Penguin(name: Str),
}
```

Lions have age. Elephants have age and weight. Penguins have a name instead of age.

### Creating Animals

```xiom
let simba = Animal.Lion(3);
let dumbo = Animal.Elephant(5, 2500.0);
let skipper = Animal.Penguin("Skipper");
```

### Describing Any Animal

Match destructures each variant and builds a description:

```xiom
fn describe(a: Animal) -> Str {
  match a {
    Lion(age) => { return "Lion, age " + age; }
    Elephant(age, weight) => { return "Elephant, age " + age + ", " + weight + "kg"; }
    Penguin(name) => { return "Penguin named " + name; }
  }
}
```

### Checking for Babies

Animals with an age field under 2 are babies. Penguins don't carry age, so they're never babies:

```xiom
fn is_baby(a: Animal) -> Bool {
  match a {
    Lion(age) => { return age < 2; }
    Elephant(age, _) => { return age < 2; }
    Penguin(_) => { return false; }
  }
}
```

The `_` in `Elephant(age, _)` means "I don't need the weight, just give me the age."

### Using the Functions

```xiom
is_baby(simba);     // false (age 3)
is_baby(dumbo);     // false (age 5)

let cub = Animal.Lion(1);
is_baby(cub);       // true (age 1)
```

